entially their entire stack)\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\n- 9-3 suited is a weak hand, ranked near the bottom of starting hands in Texas Hold'em\n- Against a random hand, 9-3 suited has roughly 32-35% equity\n- Against any premium hand (AA, KK, QQ, AK, etc.), my equity drops to ~20-30%\n- The suited aspect gives me flush potential but is marginal value\n\n**Opponent's Range Analysis:**\nAn all-in raise on the first hand is an extremely aggressive move that typically indicates either:\n1. A premium hand (AA, KK, QQ, AK) - most likely\n2. A bluff/aggressive play - less likely but possible\n3. Medium pairs trying to avoid tough post-flop decisions\n\n**Pot Odds:**\n- I need to call 19,849 more chips (already posted 50 big blind)\n- Pot would be ~39,900 chips\n- I'm getting roughly 2:1 pot odds\n- I need ~33% equity to break even on the call\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\n- This is hand 7 of 20, still early in the match\n- I'm down 600 chips cumulatively (manageable)\n- Calling and losing means I'm eliminated from this hand with 0 chips\n- My hand likely has 25-35% equity against opponent's range\n\nReason:\nWhile I'm getting approximately 2:1 pot odds which might justify a call with 33%+ equity, 9-3 suited is simply too weak to risk my entire stack this early in the match. Against an all-in raise, my opponent almost certainly has a stronger hand. Even in the best-case scenario where they're bluffing, I'm likely only a slight favorite. The risk of elimination from this hand with such a marginal holding isn't worth it when there are 13 more hands to play. Preservation of chips and waiting for better spots is the correct strategy.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
